node_modules/
.DS_Store
*.log
.env*

# wrangler local cache (holds the Cloudflare account id) - never commit
.wrangler/

# KB/07 A7 — NO SUBSCRIBER DATA MAY EVER ENTER THIS REPO.
# This repo is PUBLIC, the Pages build output is "/" (DEPLOY.md), DEPLOY.md
# instructs `git add -A`, and a static auth token was already committed here
# once and had to be killed (weekly-send.js:11). The lead bundles are the paid
# product: unmasked addresses and contractor names for every permit.
# build_bundle.py writes them to PermitPulse/data/bundles - OUTSIDE this repo -
# which is the real control. These lines are the belt to that pair of braces:
# they catch a bundle copied or generated into the lander tree by hand.
# NOTE: deliberately NOT a bare `*.html` - the root holds ~40 public SEO pages
# named like roofing-leads-greater-boston.html, and ignoring those would silently
# stop deploying most of the site.
*.zip
out/
data/bundles/
latest-weekly.html
latest-monthly.html
MassPermits-*.html
MassPermits-*.csv

# The R2 money-path objects, when pulled into this tree for inspection.
# `npx wrangler r2 object get ... --file subscribers.json` run from this
# directory is the normal way to read them, and on 2026-09-06 every one of these
# was sitting UNTRACKED AND UNIGNORED in this working tree while DEPLOY.md:20,
# DEPLOY.md:62 and AUTODELIVERY_SETUP.md:73 all instruct
# `git add -A && git commit && git push`. One such push publishes, to a PUBLIC repo:
#   subscribers.json   every buyer's email, name, Stripe customer id AND their
#                      /api/my-leads download token — the token is the auth for the
#                      paid product, so that is credential disclosure, not only PII
#   delivery-log.json  recipient addresses (`to`)
#   feed-send-log.json recipient addresses (`sent[].to`)
# None had ever been committed, so these lines close the hole outright with no
# history rewrite. Ignoring them changes nothing about the deployed site: R2 is
# the authoritative store and the Pages Functions read it through the BUNDLES
# binding, never from disk.
subscribers.json
delivery-log.json
feed-send-log.json
last-send-attempt.json
funnel-metrics.json
refresh-status.json
# The lifecycle rollup written by functions/api/engagement.js. It deliberately
# holds NO email, name or full token — only an 8-hex token prefix, a signup
# date and per-customer behaviour — so a leak here would not be credential
# disclosure the way subscribers.json would. It is still every paying
# customer's usage history, and it gets pulled into this directory by the same
# `wrangler r2 object get --file` habit as the objects above.
engagement.json

# THE ENGINE. PermitPulse ships to R2 as engine.tar.gz and NEVER via git - that is
# the whole point of the two-repo split, and the tarball is the scraping moat.
# Reading it is normally done with
#   npx wrangler r2 object get masspermits-bundles/engine.tar.gz --file engine.tar.gz --remote
# run FROM THIS DIRECTORY, which drops the private engine into a PUBLIC repo whose own
# DEPLOY.md:20, DEPLOY.md:62 and AUTODELIVERY_SETUP.md:73 all say `git add -A`. On
# 2026-09-19 an 859,545-byte engine.tar.gz was found sitting here untracked AND
# unignored. Same hole as the subscriber objects above, same fix, no history rewrite
# needed because it has never been committed. Ignoring it changes nothing about the
# deployed site: Pages never reads the engine, the refresh job fetches it from R2
# through /api/get-engine.
engine.tar.gz
engine/
engine-*/

# actionability.json. 179KB of per-lead SELL / THIN / DO NOT SELL verdicts, 283 of them
# DO NOT SELL, read by /leads/account for signed-in subscribers only. It was found on
# 2026-09-20 sitting UNTRACKED AND UNIGNORED in this PUBLIC repo (api.github.com returns
# HTTP 200 unauthenticated), whose own DEPLOY.md:20, DEPLOY.md:62 and
# AUTODELIVERY_SETUP.md:73 all instruct `git add -A`. Same hole the engine lines above
# close, still open for this file. Never committed, so no history rewrite is needed.
actionability.json
